.TH EUCLID-CLI-EKM-REVOKE-KEY 1 "August 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.33" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-ekm-revoke-key \- revoke an encryption key
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli ekm revoke-key
.BR \-\-key | \-k
.I ERN
.SH DESCRIPTION
Revokes a key: its status becomes
.BR REVOKED .
A revoked key can no longer be used to encrypt
(see
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-encrypt (1)),
but
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-decrypt (1)
keeps working, so data already encrypted under the key stays
recoverable.
.PP
Unlike
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-delete-key (1),
revoking does not schedule the key for removal - it stays around
(and decryptable) indefinitely, unless separately deleted. There is
no
.B unrevoke
action; revocation is permanent, the same as revoking a certificate or
an API credential.
.PP
Revoking a key that is already
.B PENDING_DELETION
(scheduled via
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-delete-key (1))
is refused: encryption is already blocked in that state, and
downgrading the status to
.B REVOKED
would hide the fact that a deletion is scheduled from anything that
reads status without also checking the key's
.B deletionDate
field in
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-list-keys (1).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-key " " \fIERN\fR
Euclid resource name of the key to revoke, as returned by the
.B ern
field of
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-create-key (1)
or
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-list-keys (1).
Required.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The key was revoked; its ERN, name and new status are printed as
JSON.
.TP
.B 1
Missing/invalid arguments, the server could not be reached, or the
server rejected the request (e.g. unknown ERN, or the key is already
pending deletion).
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 ekm revoke-key \e
    --key ern:ekm:eu-central-1:000000000000:key:3f1c2b7a-...
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-ekm (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-create-key (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-list-keys (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-delete-key (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-encrypt (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-decrypt (1)
